+## 2016-03-23 · `EDGcpfe/16965`
+
+**Position range for generated operator calls**
+
+*C-score +2*
+
+```text
+When the use of an operator results in the call of an overloaded operator,
+the source position range (see an_expr_node::expr_range) is recorded in the
+topmost node of the IL representing the operator invocation.  This node is
+not always the actual call node, because of transformations on the return
+type of the operator function or temporaries created to hold the return
+value.  In such cases, the source position range is now also recorded in the
+actual call node.  For example:
+
+  struct S {
+    S(S const&);
+    S operator++(int);
+  };
+  void g(S s) {
+    s++;
+  }
+
+The expression "s++" results in an IL tree where the topmost node is an
+enk_temp_init node representing the allocation and initialization of a
+temporary for the return value of the operator++ function: That node records
+the source range of the expression "s++".  The actual call node (an operator
+node of kind eok_dot_member_call) previously did not record a source range,
+but now it records the same range as the topmost node.
+```
