+## 2008-04-29
+
+**GNU C compatibility: Redeclarations and transparent unions**
+
+*C-score +4*
+
+```text
+In GNU C mode, a function can be redeclared with a parameter type that is a
+transparent union type with a field type that matches the corresponding
+parameter type in the original declaration (see Changes entry of 6/20/02).
+Previously, the composite type resulting from such a redeclaration was always
+the transparent union.  Now, if the redeclaration is not a definition, the
+original type is retained.  For example:
+
+  union __attribute((transparent_union)) TU { int i; int *p; };
+  void f(int i);            // First declaration.
+  void f(union TU tu);      // Redeclaration, type "int" retained.
+  void g(int x) { f(&x); }  // Warning: int*->int conversion.
+  void f(union TU tu) {}    // Definition, type "tu" retained.
+  void h(int x) { f(&x); }  // No warning, no conversion.
+
+This brings the front end behavior closer to that of GCC's, but GCC appears to
+retain the original type even when the redeclaration is a definition.  We do
+not implement that GCC behavior to avoid excessive disruption of internal
+structure of the front end.
+```
